another heartbreaking decision for the  hillsborough families  a last chance at securing further  criminal convictions slipped away today  when the judge in this latest trial  ruled there was no case to be heard  what a very sad day today is for the  justice system of this country  32 years the families have been fighting  to get to the truth and accountability  for 96 innocent victims who lost their  lives at hillsborough  two former police officers and a  solicitor had been on trial for  perverting the course of justice  they had denied accusations that they  changed police statements to minimize  blame on south yorkshire police  following the disaster at the fa cup  semi-final in 1989  in which 96 liverpool fans died  former shadow home secretary andy  burnham said echoes of hillsborough run  through victims of  other tragedies as he called for changes  to the law  how much of the families given to this  fight  it has to be about changing the way  things are  if they're going to level up this  country you need to level up ordinary  citizens when they walk into a courtroom  against the authorities of this country  at the moment it's a massive  mismatch today's decision means that  even though a previous inquest had ruled  that the 96  were unlawfully killed only one person  has ever been held to account  and that was for a single safety offence  after a trial in 2019  and despite the trial collapsing the  crown prosecution service defended the  decision to prosecute  saying the cps was right to bring this  case and for a court to hear the  evidence of what happened in the  aftermath of the hillsborough disaster  mary and brenda both lost their sons in  the tragedy  i don't think we'll ever have closure  and for what we've been through and  by today as well we can't appeal on that  decision  so for me there's no closure  and we'll still be going up and putting  flowers on the grave and it just brings  all the horrible memories that you've  been trying to forget forgotten it was  that many years  and it brings everything back to the  surface and you just feel  so angry both women say the truth of  what happened that day is clear  even if justice is harder to achieve  sadia chowdhury sky news in liverpool
 

